### 1. Auth guard recreates GoRouter on every auth state change — navigation stack is lost

**File:** `.agents/skills/flutter-setup-declarative-routing/SKILL.md` ~line 38

The skill teaches:

```dart
@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)
GoRouter router(Ref ref) {
  final authState = ref.watch(authStateProvider);
  return GoRouter(initialLocation: '/', redirect: ..., routes: [...]);
}
```

`keepAlive: true` prevents disposal when listener count drops to zero, but does **not** prevent re-execution when a watched dependency changes. When `authStateProvider` emits (login/logout), Riverpod invalidates `routerProvider` and re-runs its body — constructing a brand-new `GoRouter`. Flutter's `Router.didUpdateWidget` detects that `routeInformationProvider.value` changed (the new one starts at `/`), calls `setNewRoutePath('/')`, and wipes the navigation stack. The user is returned to the home screen on every auth state change.

**Fix:** Keep one `GoRouter` instance alive and drive re-evaluation via `refreshListenable`:

```dart
@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)
GoRouter router(Ref ref) {
  final notifier = ref.watch(authStateProvider.notifier);
  return GoRouter(
    initialLocation: '/',
    refreshListenable: notifier, // notifier must extend ChangeNotifier
    redirect: (context, state) {
      final isLoggedIn = notifier.isAuthenticated;
      // ...
    },
    routes: [...],
  );
}
```

